    content: `The kidney forms urine by three distinct, sequential processes — and it very much does do them as separate steps. (1) GLOMERULAR FILTRATION happens at the start of each nephron: blood entering the glomerulus (a knot of capillaries) is under relatively high pressure, and that pressure drives a "filtrate" of plasma — water, electrolytes, glucose, amino acids, urea, creatinine, vitamins, small drugs — out through the filtration barrier into Bowman's capsule and down the tubule. Blood cells and plasma proteins are too big (and repelled by the barrier's negative charge), so they stay in the blood. Total filtration across both kidneys is about 180 litres per day — the entire plasma volume is filtered ~60 times a day. (2) TUBULAR REABSORPTION happens all along the tubule that follows (proximal convoluted tubule, loop of Henle, distal convoluted tubule, collecting duct): as the filtrate flows through, the cells lining the tubule selectively transport the valuable contents BACK out of the tubule and into the peritubular capillaries — roughly 99% of the filtered water, essentially 100% of the glucose and amino acids (in health), and most of the sodium, chloride, bicarbonate, calcium, and phosphate. This is why, of the 180 L filtered, only ~1–2 L leaves as urine. Reabsorption is regulated by hormones (aldosterone for Na+, ADH for water, PTH for calcium). (3) TUBULAR SECRETION is the reverse of reabsorption: certain substances are actively transported FROM the peritubular blood INTO the tubular fluid to be excreted — hydrogen ions (for acid-base balance), potassium, ammonium, urate, creatinine, and many drugs and toxins (penicillin, diuretics, many others). So: amount of a substance in the final urine = amount filtered − amount reabsorbed + amount secreted. "Renal clearance" measures the effective volume of plasma the kidney fully clears of a substance per minute.`,
