    content: `The renin–angiotensin–aldosterone system (RAAS) is a hormonal cascade, initiated by the kidney, whose job is to defend blood pressure and blood (extracellular fluid) volume when either falls. THE CASCADE: (1) TRIGGER — specialised "juxtaglomerular" (granular) cells in the wall of the afferent arteriole of each nephron release the enzyme RENIN in response to: a drop in renal perfusion pressure (sensed directly by the cells), a fall in sodium chloride delivery to the "macula densa" of the distal tubule, or sympathetic nervous stimulation of β1-adrenoceptors on the cells (as in haemorrhage or standing up). (2) RENIN cleaves ANGIOTENSINOGEN (a protein made continuously by the liver and circulating in the blood) to produce the inactive decapeptide ANGIOTENSIN I. (3) ANGIOTENSIN-CONVERTING ENZYME (ACE), found on the endothelium especially of the lung and kidney capillaries, snips two amino acids off angiotensin I to make the active octapeptide ANGIOTENSIN II. ANGIOTENSIN II'S EFFECTS (all raise BP / restore volume): potent constriction of systemic arterioles (raising vascular resistance and BP directly); stimulation of the adrenal cortex (zona glomerulosa) to secrete ALDOSTERONE, which acts on the distal tubule and collecting duct to reabsorb sodium (water follows) and excrete potassium and hydrogen ions; stimulation of ADH (vasopressin) release from the posterior pituitary and of the thirst centre; enhanced sodium reabsorption in the proximal tubule; preferential constriction of the glomerular efferent arteriole (which props up glomerular filtration pressure when renal blood flow is low); and, chronically, growth and fibrosis of blood vessels and the heart ("remodelling"). CLINICAL: because an overactive RAAS worsens hypertension and heart failure and damages the kidney and heart, it is heavily drugged — ACE inhibitors ("-pril"), angiotensin-receptor blockers / ARBs ("-sartan"), direct renin inhibitors (aliskiren), and mineralocorticoid-receptor antagonists (spironolactone, eplerenone). Aldosterone excess from an adrenal tumour is Conn's syndrome (primary hyperaldosteronism), a curable cause of hypertension.`,
