    content: `The GLOMERULAR FILTRATION RATE (GFR) is the volume of plasma that the glomeruli of both kidneys filter into the tubules per minute. A healthy young adult has a GFR of about 90–120 mL/min/1.73 m² (indexed to a standard body surface area). GFR is the best single measure of overall kidney function, because it reflects the total working nephron mass; it declines naturally with age (roughly 1 mL/min per year after ~40) and drops in kidney disease. Chronic kidney disease (CKD) is defined and STAGED by GFR (plus markers of damage): G1 ≥90 (with damage), G2 60–89, G3a 45–59, G3b 30–44, G4 15–29, and G5 <15 = kidney failure (needing dialysis or transplant). A "true" GFR is measured by injecting a substance that is only filtered (inulin, or radiolabelled markers) — impractical for routine use — so in practice GFR is ESTIMATED ("eGFR") using equations (CKD-EPI, MDRD) that take the blood CREATININE concentration and adjust for age and sex. CREATININE is a breakdown product of creatine phosphate in muscle, generated at a fairly steady daily rate, freely filtered at the glomerulus, and only slightly secreted and not reabsorbed — so as filtering capacity falls, creatinine backs up in the blood and its level rises. But it is an imperfect marker: (1) it depends on MUSCLE MASS — a frail 80-year-old or an amputee makes little creatinine, so a "normal-looking" creatinine can conceal badly reduced function, while a young bodybuilder may have a high creatinine with perfectly good kidneys; (2) it is insensitive early — creatinine often stays in the normal range until roughly half of kidney function has already been lost ("creatinine-blind" range); (3) it is affected by diet (a big meat meal), some drugs (trimethoprim, cimetidine block its secretion), and hydration. Cystatin C, a protein made by all nucleated cells at a constant rate, is a muscle-independent alternative used to refine the estimate. A sudden rise in creatinine over hours to days = acute kidney injury.`,
