    content: `The kidneys keep body water constant not by changing filtration but by finely adjusting how much of the filtered water they REABSORB versus let go as urine, and the master control is the hormone ADH (antidiuretic hormone, also called arginine vasopressin, AVP). ADH is synthesised by neurons in the hypothalamus (the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei), transported down their axons, and STORED in and RELEASED from the posterior pituitary gland — it is not "released by the hypothalamus," and it is definitely not renin. RELEASE is triggered by: (1) OSMORECEPTORS in the hypothalamus that sense a rise in plasma osmolality (blood becoming more concentrated) of as little as 1–2% — from water loss (sweating, vomiting, diarrhoea, inadequate drinking) or a salt load; and (2) BARORECEPTORS in the heart and great vessels that sense a fall in blood volume or pressure of ~10%+ (haemorrhage, dehydration). ACTION: ADH travels to the kidney and binds V2 receptors on the cells of the collecting ducts, causing them to insert AQUAPORIN-2 water channels into the tubule-facing membrane. Water then flows osmotically out of the dilute tubular fluid, across those channels, into the surrounding kidney medulla — which the loop of Henle's countercurrent multiplier keeps extremely concentrated (up to ~1200 mOsm) — and from there back into the blood. The result is a small volume of highly concentrated urine and retained body water. When the blood is dilute (you've drunk a lot), ADH secretion is suppressed, the aquaporins are removed, the collecting duct becomes water-impermeable, and the water that was reabsorbed in earlier tubule segments is not recaptured — you produce a large volume of pale, dilute urine. ADH also constricts blood vessels at higher concentrations (hence "vasopressin"), supporting BP in severe volume loss. DISORDERS: too little ADH (central diabetes insipidus, from pituitary damage) or kidney resistance to it (nephrogenic DI) → uncontrollable production of huge volumes of dilute urine and constant thirst. Too much ADH (SIADH — from lung disease, brain injury, drugs, some cancers) → water retention and dangerously low blood sodium (hyponatraemia).`,
