    content: `The countercurrent multiplier is the loop-of-Henle mechanism that allows the kidney to concentrate urine well above the concentration of blood and thereby conserve water — and it genuinely operates in the loop of Henle (heat exchange in a bird's or a wading animal's legs is a separate, simpler example of the countercurrent principle). SETUP: the loop of Henle is a hairpin. Its DESCENDING limb is permeable to water but not to salt. Its ASCENDING limb is the opposite — impermeable to water, and its thick upper part actively PUMPS sodium, potassium, and chloride OUT of the tubular fluid into the surrounding kidney tissue (the medullary "interstitium"). THE "SINGLE EFFECT": that active salt pumping can only create a modest osmotic difference — about 200 mOsm/kg — between the fluid inside the ascending limb and the interstitium just outside it, at any one horizontal level. THE MULTIPLICATION: because the descending and ascending limbs sit right next to each other running in OPPOSITE directions ("countercurrent"), and because tubular fluid is continuously flowing around the hairpin, that small 200 mOsm "single effect" is progressively added up (multiplied) along the vertical length of the loop. Fluid descending gets more and more concentrated as water is drawn out into the increasingly salty medulla; fluid in the ascending limb gets more and more dilute as salt is pumped out. The net result is a large standing osmotic gradient in the kidney medulla — from ~300 mOsm/kg (isotonic to plasma) in the outer cortex to ~1200 mOsm/kg deep in the inner medulla. UREA recycling from the collecting duct contributes roughly half of this deep gradient. THE PAYOFF: the collecting duct carries the tubular fluid back down through this hyperosmotic medulla on its way to the ureter. When ADH is present and has opened the collecting duct's aquaporin channels, water is osmotically sucked out of the collecting-duct fluid into the concentrated medulla, so the urine that finally emerges can be as concentrated as ~1200 mOsm/kg (very little water lost). When ADH is absent, the collecting duct stays water-tight and dilute urine (~50 mOsm/kg) is passed. The blood vessels of the medulla (the "vasa recta") loop in the same hairpin shape and use passive COUNTERCURRENT EXCHANGE to supply the medulla with oxygen and remove reabsorbed water without carrying away and dissipating the precious salt gradient.`,
