    content: `Urination (micturition) is governed by a reflex loop between the bladder and the sacral spinal cord, which in a continent adult is held under conscious control by the brain. (Oxytocin plays no role — that was a wrong answer.) COMPONENTS: the DETRUSOR (the smooth muscle in the bladder wall, which contracts to empty it — parasympathetic, pelvic nerves, S2–S4); the INTERNAL urethral sphincter (smooth muscle, involuntary, sympathetic — kept closed during filling); and the EXTERNAL urethral sphincter (skeletal muscle of the pelvic floor, VOLUNTARY, pudendal nerve — you can consciously squeeze or relax it). STORAGE PHASE: as urine trickles in from the ureters, the bladder wall stretches, and STRETCH RECEPTORS fire afferent signals up the pelvic nerves to the sacral cord. At low volumes, spinal and brainstem reflexes (the "guarding reflex") keep the detrusor relaxed (sympathetic + local) and both sphincters closed, so the bladder fills passively and painlessly. VOIDING PHASE: once the volume passes ~300–400 mL, the afferent signal reaches the brain — the periaqueductal grey relays it to the PONTINE MICTURITION CENTRE and to the cortex, producing the conscious sensation of needing to go. If the moment is inappropriate, the cortex actively inhibits the pontine micturition centre and tells the external sphincter to stay contracted. When it is appropriate to void, the cortex releases that inhibition; the pontine micturition centre then coordinates a sustained parasympathetic contraction of the detrusor together with relaxation of BOTH sphincters, and urine flows. In INFANTS (before toilet training) and after SPINAL CORD INJURY above the sacral level, the brain is disconnected from the loop, and micturition becomes a pure automatic spinal reflex — the bladder empties reflexly whenever it reaches a threshold fullness, without warning or control ("neurogenic bladder"). Overactive bladder, stress incontinence, and urinary retention are disorders of different parts of this system.`,
