    content: `A kidney stone (renal calculus; the condition is nephrolithiasis, or urolithiasis anywhere in the tract) is a hard, crystalline concretion that forms in the kidney or urinary tract. THE BASIC MECHANISM: urine is a solution carrying dissolved waste salts. When the concentration of a stone-forming salt rises high enough (urine becomes "supersaturated") — because there is too little water, too much of the salt, or too little of the substances that normally keep it dissolved (like citrate) — the salt crystallises out; crystals then aggregate, often on a base of calcium-phosphate deposits in the kidney tissue ("Randall's plaques"), and grow into a stone. TYPES (by composition): CALCIUM OXALATE (~75–80% — the classic stone; promoted by low fluid intake, a high-salt or high-animal-protein diet, high dietary oxalate, low urinary citrate, and — counterintuitively — a very LOW calcium diet, which frees more oxalate to be absorbed and excreted); CALCIUM PHOSPHATE (associated with alkaline urine, renal tubular acidosis, and hyperparathyroidism); URIC ACID (~5–10% — forms in persistently acidic, concentrated urine; linked to gout, obesity, diabetes, and high purine intake; these stones don't show on plain X-ray); STRUVITE / "infection stones" (magnesium ammonium phosphate — form only when the urine is infected with bacteria that split urea and alkalinise the urine, e.g. Proteus; they grow fast and can fill the whole collecting system as a branched "staghorn calculus"); and CYSTINE (a rare autosomal-recessive transport defect, cystinuria, causing recurrent stones from childhood). RISK FACTORS: inadequate fluid intake, hot climate and occupation, obesity, type 2 diabetes, metabolic syndrome, gout, primary hyperparathyroidism, chronic diarrhoea/bariatric surgery (fat malabsorption raises oxalate), recurrent UTIs, and a strong family history. PRESENTATION: a stone sitting in the kidney may cause nothing; a stone passing down the ureter causes "renal colic" — sudden, severe, waxing-and-waning pain radiating from the loin (flank) round to the groin, with nausea, vomiting, restlessness, and blood in the urine (visible or on dipstick). Diagnosed by non-contrast CT. Most small stones (<5 mm) pass with fluids and analgesia; larger ones need shockwave lithotripsy, ureteroscopy with laser fragmentation, or surgery. PREVENTION: drink enough to make 2.5–3 L of urine a day, reduce salt, keep normal dietary calcium, moderate oxalate-rich foods and animal protein, and — depending on stone type — potassium citrate, thiazide diuretics, or allopurinol.`,
