    content: `The two adrenal (suprarenal) glands sit like triangular caps on top of each kidney, and each is really TWO endocrine organs fused together, with different embryonic origins, structures, and hormones. THE ADRENAL MEDULLA (the inner ~10%) is developmentally and functionally a modified sympathetic ganglion — its "chromaffin cells" are like sympathetic nerve endings that dump their transmitter straight into the bloodstream as a hormone. It secretes the CATECHOLAMINES adrenaline/epinephrine (about 80%) and noradrenaline/norepinephrine, on command from the sympathetic nervous system, to produce the systemic "fight-or-flight" response: increased heart rate and force, raised blood pressure, dilated airways, mobilised glucose and fatty acids, blood shunted to skeletal muscle and away from gut and skin, dilated pupils, and heightened alertness. A tumour of these cells (phaeochromocytoma) causes episodic severe hypertension, palpitations, sweating, and headache. THE ADRENAL CORTEX (the outer ~90%) synthesises STEROID hormones from cholesterol, arranged in three concentric zones that make three classes: (1) ZONA GLOMERULOSA (outermost) → ALDOSTERONE, a mineralocorticoid that makes the kidney retain sodium and water and excrete potassium — controlling extracellular fluid volume and blood pressure. It is regulated mainly by the renin–angiotensin system and by blood potassium. Excess = Conn's syndrome (hypertension + low potassium); deficiency contributes to Addisonian crisis. (2) ZONA FASCICULATA (middle, largest) → CORTISOL, a glucocorticoid and the principal "stress hormone": it raises blood glucose (gluconeogenesis, insulin antagonism), breaks down protein and fat for fuel, has powerful anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressive effects, maintains blood pressure and vascular tone, and follows a daily rhythm (highest on waking). It is controlled by ACTH from the anterior pituitary, itself driven by CRH from the hypothalamus, in a negative-feedback loop. Excess cortisol = Cushing's syndrome (central obesity, moon face, thin skin, bruising, hypertension, diabetes, osteoporosis); deficiency = part of Addison's disease. (3) ZONA RETICULARIS (innermost) → weak ANDROGENS (DHEA and androstenedione), which are converted in the tissues to testosterone and oestrogen (significant in women and around puberty). Complete loss of adrenal cortex function (Addison's disease — autoimmune, TB, or from abruptly stopping long-term steroid drugs) is life-threatening and needs lifelong hydrocortisone and fludrocortisone replacement.`,
