    content: `Normally, urine contains almost no protein — under about 150 mg per day, and under ~30 mg per day of albumin. This is because the glomerular filtration barrier has two defences: its physical pore size (which excludes large molecules) and a strong fixed NEGATIVE electrical charge on the barrier (which repels albumin, itself negatively charged). The tiny amount of protein that does slip through is then reabsorbed and broken down by the proximal tubule. PROTEINURIA (protein in the urine, more than the trace amount) signals that this system has broken down, in one of three patterns: (1) GLOMERULAR proteinuria — the filtration barrier is damaged and leaking, letting albumin (and in severe cases larger proteins) through. This is by far the commonest and most important type, caused by diabetes (diabetic nephropathy), hypertension, the various forms of glomerulonephritis, lupus, amyloidosis, and pre-eclampsia. (2) TUBULAR proteinuria — the glomerulus is fine but damaged tubules fail to reabsorb the normal filtered load of small proteins (from drugs, heavy metals, inherited tubulopathies). (3) OVERFLOW proteinuria — an abnormal small protein is produced in such excess that it floods the filter faster than the tubule can reabsorb it, classically free immunoglobulin "light chains" (Bence Jones protein) in multiple myeloma, or myoglobin in rhabdomyolysis, or haemoglobin in haemolysis. WHY IT MATTERS: (a) it is one of the EARLIEST and most sensitive markers of kidney damage — small amounts of albumin ("microalbuminuria," now called moderately increased albuminuria) appear in diabetic kidney disease years before the eGFR starts to fall, giving a window to intervene; (b) the filtered protein is itself TOXIC to the tubular cells it passes over, provoking inflammation and scarring, so proteinuria accelerates the loss of kidney function (which is why ACE inhibitors and ARBs, which reduce proteinuria, slow CKD progression); (c) the degree of proteinuria independently predicts progression to kidney failure AND cardiovascular events and death, even at low levels; (d) very heavy loss (>3.5 g/day) causes NEPHROTIC SYNDROME — low blood albumin, generalised swelling (oedema, especially around the eyes and in the legs), high cholesterol, a tendency to clot, and visibly "frothy/foamy" urine. Screened simply with a urine albumin:creatinine ratio (ACR) or protein:creatinine ratio (PCR) on a single sample.`,
