    content: `Blood pH is held tightly between 7.35 and 7.45 by buffers, the lungs, and the kidneys working together. The bicarbonate/carbonic-acid buffer system does the instant absorption; the LUNGS adjust within minutes by changing how much CO2 (a volatile acid) is exhaled; and the KIDNEYS provide the slower (hours to days) but ultimately complete correction, because only they can deal with the "fixed" (non-volatile) acids — sulfuric and phosphoric acid from protein and phospholipid metabolism, and organic acids — which the body generates at roughly 1 mmol per kg body weight per day (~70 mmol) and which the lungs cannot remove. The kidney does three jobs: (1) BICARBONATE REABSORPTION — about 4,000 mmol of bicarbonate is filtered at the glomeruli each day, and losing it in the urine would be catastrophic, so the proximal tubule (and to a lesser extent the rest of the nephron) reclaims essentially all of it, by secreting H⁺ into the lumen which combines with filtered HCO₃⁻ to form CO2 and water that diffuse back into the cell and are re-formed into bicarbonate. (2) GENERATION OF NEW BICARBONATE / net acid excretion — to replace the bicarbonate consumed buffering the day's metabolic acid load, the distal tubule and collecting duct actively secrete H⁺ into the urine using proton pumps; for every H⁺ excreted, a new HCO₃⁻ is returned to the blood. The excreted H⁺ can't just make the urine acidic without limit (urine pH bottoms out around 4.5), so it is trapped by two urinary buffers: phosphate (H⁺ + HPO₄²⁻ → H₂PO₄⁻, measured as "titratable acid") and, more importantly and adjustably, AMMONIA (the tubule cells make NH₃ from the amino acid glutamine, it captures H⁺ to become NH₄⁺, which is excreted; in a chronic acid load the kidney ramps ammonia production up several-fold). (3) In ALKALOSIS the kidney does the opposite — it stops reabsorbing all the bicarbonate and lets the excess spill into the urine (which is why the urine is alkaline after vomiting is corrected). The kidneys therefore fully correct a chronic metabolic acid load (kidney failure itself impairs this, causing chronic acidosis) and provide the renal compensation for chronic respiratory acid–base disturbances (retaining bicarbonate in chronic respiratory acidosis, dumping it in chronic respiratory alkalosis).`,
