    keywords: [
      'why do the kidneys make erythropoietin and activate vitamin d hormonal functions of the kidney',
      'besides filtering blood the kidney is an endocrine organ its peritubular interstitial cells sense the oxygen content of the blood passing through when oxygen delivery is low anaemia altitude blood loss chronic hypoxia they secrete the hormone erythropoietin epo',
      'epo travels to the bone marrow and stimulates the production and maturation of red blood cells this is why chronic kidney disease causes anaemia treated with synthetic epo drugs and why epo is abused as a doping agent',
      'the kidney also performs the final activation step of vitamin d the enzyme 1 alpha hydroxylase converts inactive 25 hydroxyvitamin d made by the liver into the active hormone calcitriol 1 25 dihydroxyvitamin d which raises calcium and phosphate absorption from the gut so kidney failure also causes low blood calcium and renal bone disease',
    ],
    content: `The kidney is not only a filter — it is also an endocrine gland with two important hormonal roles beyond its release of renin. (1) ERYTHROPOIETIN (EPO). Specialised fibroblast-like cells in the kidney's peritubular interstitium (in the cortex, near the tubules) act as the body's oxygen sensor for red-cell production. They continuously monitor the oxygen content of the blood flowing past — which reflects both the arterial oxygen level and the haemoglobin concentration. When oxygen delivery falls (anaemia, chronic lung disease, living at high altitude, blood loss, or certain haemoglobin problems), these cells stabilise a transcription factor (HIF) and increase their output of the hormone ERYTHROPOIETIN, which is released into the blood and travels to the BONE MARROW. There it binds receptors on red-cell precursors, protecting them from programmed death and driving their proliferation and maturation, so red-cell production rises over days to weeks and oxygen-carrying capacity recovers. Consequences: (a) chronic kidney disease destroys these EPO-producing cells, so a hallmark of advanced CKD is a normocytic anaemia — treated with injectable synthetic EPO ("erythropoiesis-stimulating agents") and iron; (b) recombinant EPO and "altitude training" both work by this pathway, and EPO is a notorious blood-doping agent. (2) ACTIVATION OF VITAMIN D. Vitamin D (from skin sunlight exposure or diet) is biologically inert and must be hydroxylated twice: first in the LIVER to 25-hydroxyvitamin D (calcidiol — the form measured in a blood test), and then in the KIDNEY, where the enzyme 1-alpha-hydroxylase (in the proximal tubule cells, stimulated by parathyroid hormone and low phosphate) adds the final hydroxyl group to make the active hormone CALCITRIOL (1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D). Calcitriol acts mainly on the gut to increase absorption of dietary calcium and phosphate, and it supports normal bone mineralisation and calcium balance. Consequence: in chronic kidney disease this activation step fails, so despite adequate vitamin D intake the patient develops low blood calcium, secondary hyperparathyroidism, and "renal osteodystrophy" (weak, painful bones and vascular calcification) — managed with "active" vitamin D analogues (alfacalcidol, calcitriol, paricalcitol) that bypass the missing kidney step, plus phosphate binders.`,
